WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.399
- I'm sort of thinking about
all of the people that I know.

00:00:03.399 --> 00:00:07.060
That I have a relationship
and a history with.

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:10.090
And people don't know
them like I know them.

00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:11.890
You know, I mean Gwyneth Paltrow,

00:00:11.890 --> 00:00:13.940
who's one of my early guests,

00:00:13.940 --> 00:00:15.389
will be when this comes out,

00:00:15.389 --> 00:00:18.891
I've known her since she
was sixteen years old.

00:00:18.891 --> 00:00:19.724
- Wow!

00:00:19.724 --> 00:00:22.250
- When my wife and I were first together,

00:00:22.250 --> 00:00:24.520
Gwyneth was living in my guest bedroom,

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:26.230
trying to get an agent.

00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:28.780
So, I'm going to be
able to talk to people.

00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:30.730
- That's a crazy story.

00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:32.620
- Yeah yeah, but that's,

00:00:32.620 --> 00:00:33.740
just the beginning.

00:00:33.740 --> 00:00:36.280
You know, whether it's Alec Baldwin,

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:40.520
and the time he tried to
get me sober on the beach.

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:43.530
When I was at the height
of my crazy partying,

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:46.908
or you know, whether it's Magic Johnson.

00:00:46.908 --> 00:00:51.908
Reminiscing about the fact
that Pat Reilly banned me,

00:00:52.030 --> 00:00:54.313
from staying at the same
hotel as the Lakers.

00:00:54.313 --> 00:00:57.960
I mean, it's been 40 years of doing this.

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:00.720
And you know, I get Mike Myers on,

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:03.320
or I get Chris Pratt on.

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:06.540
And we're gonna have a
connection and a history.

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:08.429
That no one else is gonna have.

00:01:08.429 --> 00:01:10.670
- How do you break down their walls?

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:12.840
Cause a lot of people get reserved,

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:14.460
you know or they get
kind of like calculated,

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:15.530
cause they're like, am
I supposed to say this,

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:16.370
or not supposed to say this.

00:01:16.370 --> 00:01:18.320
And there's that kinship,
that friendship that I think

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:21.610
is really important that your
podcast is gonna bring out.

00:01:21.610 --> 00:01:23.850
A side of people we haven't really seen.

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:26.690
But speaking of all those times,

00:01:26.690 --> 00:01:29.270
wasn't there a story also
about you and Tom Cruise?

00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:31.150
Spending the night together
in a stranger's house?

00:01:31.150 --> 00:01:32.990
Cause I need to know more about that.

00:01:32.990 --> 00:01:34.190
- Yeah, well the way you say it,

00:01:34.190 --> 00:01:35.778
makes it more interesting.

00:01:35.778 --> 00:01:36.909
- Yeah.

00:01:36.909 --> 00:01:38.738
- Almost an even better story.

00:01:38.738 --> 00:01:39.820
(Kelly laughs)

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:43.400
What I want, with just that
headline and no context,

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:45.566
I'm imagining such--

00:01:45.566 --> 00:01:47.700
- I have gone to so many places,

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:49.190
and I'm into any of them.

00:01:49.190 --> 00:01:51.370
- Yeah, well for sure.

00:01:51.370 --> 00:01:52.500
So no, what happened was,

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:54.663
when we were doing The Outsiders,

00:01:55.520 --> 00:01:57.960
we're 18 and 19 years old,

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:00.810
it's Francis Ford Coppola, the director.

00:02:00.810 --> 00:02:03.880
Who directed The Godfather
and Apocalypse Now,

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:07.310
and the most intense kind
of director imaginable.

00:02:07.310 --> 00:02:09.286
And in his effort to

00:02:09.286 --> 00:02:13.520
make us more authentic as Greasers,

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:18.058
as sort of tough Tulsa sort of,

00:02:18.058 --> 00:02:21.950
you know, wrong-side-of-the-tracks guys,

00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:25.890
he found a bunch of
different actual Greasers,

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:26.990
who were now grown-up adults,

00:02:26.990 --> 00:02:30.700
and made us go spend the
night and live with them.

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:31.930
They came to us at rehearsal,

00:02:31.930 --> 00:02:33.620
and so, we're gonna divide you up.

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:36.060
Rob, Tom, you guys are gonna go stay with

00:02:36.950 --> 00:02:40.530
Bill and Sandy Webber.

00:02:40.530 --> 00:02:43.618
And we just went to their
little house and had dinner.

00:02:43.618 --> 00:02:45.400
- Oh my Gosh, this could've gone

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:47.590
very Dateline Special though.

00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:48.569
Like you know--

00:02:48.569 --> 00:02:51.750
- Oh Tom and I ended up in the basement.

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:54.160
In two cots.

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:58.840
And we're like, we
don't know these people.

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:00.646
I mean, who vetted them?

00:03:00.646 --> 00:03:04.406
You think Francis Ford
Coppola spent a lot of time

00:03:04.406 --> 00:03:05.840
vetting these people?

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:07.410
I can tell you, he didn't.

00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:09.168
He was with an espresso,

00:03:09.168 --> 00:03:12.070
watching some fancy Fellini movie,

00:03:12.070 --> 00:03:13.150
and he went, yeah yeah that sounds great.

00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:14.950
And went back to his espresso.

00:03:14.950 --> 00:03:15.860
- Meanwhile, you had to live it.

00:03:15.860 --> 00:03:19.080
- At two o'clock in the
morning in their basement.

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:19.990
- That's amazing.

00:03:19.990 --> 00:03:21.200
It's an awesome story.

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:23.778
I will listen to your
podcast for this story alone.

00:03:23.778 --> 00:03:26.873
Cause it's scary and I
would've been like, I'm out.

